From fd6388c2f3fccc9e5bbc21e1b203365bd328a08d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xFFshell <0xFFshell@users.noreply.gitlab.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 13:11:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] humanize: hold the crouch across a crossing, and charge it

Every key is cleared and re-decided from scratch every tick, and the traverse
asked for the crouch only on the ticks where the block being stepped onto was
missing and its placement was ready. A bridge was therefore walked
crouch-stand-crouch-stand, once per block: the four or five ticks of walking onto
the block that had just gone in were walked upright, at walk speed, and then the
crouch went back down for the next one. Nobody crosses a gap that way.

CarefulFooting is the rule, and it is a stance rather than a timer: a movement
that has to place the block it is stepping onto asks for the crouch
(Movement#isPlacingUnderItself), it stays down while the block under the step
after this one has no floor either (Movement#isAnotherGapAhead - the middle of a
crossing is a block placed with the next gap already in front of the feet), and
it comes up on the tick there is solid ground under the feet and ahead. Nothing
is timed and nothing is drawn: the crossing ends where the ground does, which is
when a person stands up too. It never shortens an ask, and it never invents one -
a walk that crosses nothing gets no crouch however many holes are beside it,
because the gap ahead is only read while the crouch is already down. Asked for
once a tick from Movement.update(), the seam every movement passes through, and
held by the input layer next to KeyBeat for the same reason KeyBeat is held
there: the walk onto a just-placed block is a tick the movement boundary is in
the middle of. assumeSafeWalk, the user's own "do not crouch", is honoured.

The block is then priced as it is walked. The side-place branch of
MovementTraverse#cost was returning a plain walk cost for a block the bot walks
crouched, while its own updateState sets the crouch for the side-place path as
well as the backplace one - and the backplace branch has charged
SNEAK_ONE_BLOCK_COST / WALK_ONE_BLOCK_COST since it was written. A crouched walk
priced as an upright one is a cost that does not describe the path, which is the
one thing the humanlike work here is not allowed to do. This is the change Plover
withdrew a hangover-fix in favour of at 12:47 UTC: the cadence and the price land
together, or not at all.

Validation: ./gradlew :test - 39 classes, 318 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors,
0 skipped (307 before; the eleven new are CarefulFootingTest's eight and
BridgeCrossingTest's three). Both new tests were checked for teeth rather than
assumed: removing the multiplier from the side-place branch fails the price pin
naming the branch; making the crouch a function of the ask alone fails the
crossing tests with "the bot stood up in the middle of its own crossing"; and
deleting the traverse's answer to the ask fails the seam pin. One vacuous pass
was found and fixed on the way: the seam pin skipped instead of failing when its
method was missing, because it used an assumption where it needed an assertion.

Limitation: the wiring needs a running game, as LookBehavior's does. What to
watch: cross a gap and the bot should be crouched from the edge to the far side
and upright on the first solid block after it; stand at the edge of a two-deep
drop without bridging and it should not crouch at all.
